{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS000611","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All 20 candidates are direct matches to urinary bladder cancer (trait_reported is bladder cancer and trait_efo is urinary bladder carcinoma/cancer; phenotyping_reported is generally bladder cancer/cancer of bladder/incident bladder cancer or closely aligned urothelial carcinoma). Across the candidates, the most PRS-comparable performance evidence is limited: most report only full-model AUROC (often with covariates) or effect sizes without a PRS-only AUROC/R², so cross-model ranking is uncertain.

PGS000611 is the best-supported choice from the visible evidence because it comes from a coherent bladder-cancer PRSweb family with the same endpoint (“Cancer of bladder”), basic covariates (age, sex, batch, PCs 1–4), and it shows the strongest risk stratification signal among its close siblings (Odds Ratio top 1% vs rest = 2.66 [1.79–3.93]) along with a moderate per-SD effect size (OR=1.284 [1.211–1.361]) and a similar full-model AUROC (0.567) to the best in-family alternatives. While PGS000610/PGS000612 show slightly higher/full-model AUROC (0.572) and similar per-SD OR (~1.292), they lack the stronger top-1% stratification seen in PGS000611.

Main limitation: the reported AUROCs are full-model and not explicitly PRS-only, making them non-comparable across studies with different covariate sets; additionally, there is meaningful competition from other direct-match models (e.g., PGS000071/PGS000782 and PGS000723) with very high full-model AUROC/C-index but with heavier risk-factor covariates and/or potential non-comparable reporting, so the overall selection remains only moderately confident."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS002738","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All four candidates are direct matches in concept, but they split into two endpoint/label clusters: (1) PGS000201/PGS000202 are labeled “Problematic alcohol use” with EFO “alcohol dependence measurement” while their phenotyping_reported is DSM-5 alcohol use disorder criteria count; (2) PGS002738/PGS002739 are explicitly “Alcohol use disorder” with trait_efo “alcohol dependence.” Given domain guidance that phenotyping_reported is primary for endpoint fidelity, the AUD models (PGS002738/PGS002739) align more cleanly to the target “alcohol dependence” than the “measurement/problematic use” labeling.

Between the AUD models, PGS002738 is preferred because it has a European evaluation sample (EVAL: EUR 100%) consistent with the target’s available heritability reference being EUR, and it reports a clear discriminative effect-size style metric (OR top 10% vs rest = 1.96, 95% CI 1.54–2.51) in n=7,900. PGS002739 is evaluated only in African American/Afro-Caribbean ancestry and is a gene-based PRS-CSx variant with far fewer variants (858) and a somewhat smaller top-decile OR (1.76).

Main limitation: none of the candidates report PRS-only AUROC or PRS-only R2; reported metrics are limited to ΔR2 (for PGS000201/202) or top-decile OR (for PGS002738/2739), and covariates are unknown for PGS002738/2739, which reduces comparability and keeps confidence below high."}

